# Command-line interface

The executable is `nat`. Its help shows this command shape:

```text
nat init [DIR]
nat build [--project DIR] [--proposal JSON] [--full]
nat watch [--project DIR] [--no-test] [--proposal JSON]
nat run [--project DIR] [ARGS...]
nat test [--project DIR]
nat status [--project DIR]
nat check [--project DIR]
nat promote ID [--project DIR]
```

`nat help`, `nat --help`, and `nat -h` print help. `nat version`, `nat --version`, and `nat -V` print the package version. Invalid commands, options, missing values, and excess arguments fail with a useful message and nonzero exit status.

Except for `init`, commands find the project by walking from the current directory toward its ancestors until they find `spec/`. `--project DIR` selects a project explicitly and requires that `DIR/spec` exist. Paths passed as flags can be relative to the caller's current directory.

## Commands

- `init [DIR]` defaults to the current directory. Create `spec/`, `inferred/`, and `generated/` if needed. If there are no `.nat` files, create `spec/app.nat` describing a CLI greeting by name, defaulting to the world. Do not replace existing `.nat` files. Print the project path and next step.
- `build` validates and writes a compilation. Skip the compiler when inputs and tracked outputs are current and no explicit proposal is given. `--full` forces a whole-project rebuild. `--proposal JSON` always loads and validates that saved response, even if the previous build is current; it is an explicit request to apply a proposal. Use it for deterministic or offline builds.
- `run` builds when needed and executes the generated run command from `generated/`. Forward remaining arguments to that command without a shell. A literal `--` ends nat option parsing.
- `test` builds when needed, executes all generated checks from `generated/`, and fails if there are none or if any check fails.
- `status` reports no build, stale inputs, edited/missing tracked artifacts, or a current build, along with the previous summary and file counts.
- `check` exits successfully only when specs, references, generated files, and inferred files match the last build.
- `watch` checks once at startup, then detects input changes. Poll approximately every 150 ms and wait approximately 400 ms after the last change before rebuilding. Run generated checks by default; `--no-test` skips checks. On a build/check error, report it and keep watching.
- `promote ID` accepts an existing inferred assumption from a current build, appends its statement to `spec/promoted.nat`, removes it from the inferred record, and updates the manifest so `check` still passes. Do not silently overwrite other source files.
